C = [
("h1", "9. The Blood, the Cross and the Peace"),
("p", "Claim: in Ephesians and Colossians, and in Romans 5:9, Paul speaks of the blood of Christ and of the cross in the same breath, and what he says they did is redemption, forgiveness, nearness and peace. The passages are addressed to the Body, and the volume reads them as the Body’s own. They are not the blood of the covenant spoken of in the Supper, and they do not make the Body a party to a covenant that was made with Israel."),
("sub", "9.1 Redemption and forgiveness through his blood"),
("p", "“In whom we have redemption through his blood, the forgiveness of sins, according to the riches of his grace” (Ephesians 1:7). The same words are in Colossians: “In whom we have redemption through his blood, even the forgiveness of sins” (Colossians 1:14). The two sentences are close. In both, the redemption is in him, through his blood, and it is the forgiveness of sins. The sentence in Ephesians adds that it is according to the riches of his grace. The word redemption is the subject of another study in this series, which reads it for the company, the price and the timing, and the volume cites it and does not restate it."),
("sub", "9.2 “Made nigh by the blood of Christ” (Ephesians 2:13–16)"),
("p", "“But now in Christ Jesus ye who sometimes were far off are made nigh by the blood of Christ. For he is our peace, who hath made both one, and hath broken down the middle wall of partition between us; Having abolished in his flesh the enmity, even the law of commandments contained in ordinances; for to make in himself of twain one new man, so making peace; And that he might reconcile both unto God in one body by the cross, having slain the enmity thereby” (Ephesians 2:13–16). The passage uses three of the figures of earlier chapters: blood, enmity and the cross. Blood makes nigh. The flesh of Christ abolished the enmity, and the wording is that the law of commandments contained in ordinances is abolished for the Body. The cross reconciles both unto God in one body, and the enmity is slain by it."),
("ctbl", "What Ephesians 2:13–16 says and what it leaves unsaid.", [
    ("The clause", "What it says", "What it leaves unsaid"),
    ("“ye who sometimes were far off are made nigh by the blood of Christ”", "The readers, once far off, are made nigh, and the means is the blood of Christ", "The distance in detail; the context of the chapter supplies it"),
    ("“For he is our peace, who hath made both one”", "Christ is our peace and has made both one", "The identity of the two beyond “both”; the first chapters of the letter supply them"),
    ("“Having abolished in his flesh the enmity, even the law of commandments contained in ordinances”", "The enmity is abolished in his flesh, and the enmity is explained as the law of commandments in ordinances", "That the law is abolished for any company other than the Body; the passage is addressed to the Ephesians"),
    ("“reconcile both unto God in one body by the cross”", "Both are reconciled unto God in one body, and the means is the cross", "How the cross reconciles; the clause states the means"),
    ("“having slain the enmity thereby”", "The enmity was slain by the cross", "Whether the enmity is between persons, or with God, or both; the sentence has both in view"),
  ], [0.34, 0.34, 0.32]),
("sub", "9.3 “The blood of his cross” (Colossians 1:20–22)"),
("p", "“And, having made peace through the blood of his cross, by him to reconcile all things unto himself; by him, I say, whether they be things in earth, or things in heaven. And you, that were sometime alienated and enemies in your mind by wicked works, yet now hath he reconciled In the body of his flesh through death, to present you holy and unblameable and unreproveable in his sight” (Colossians 1:20–22). The blood and the cross are joined in a single phrase, “the blood of his cross.” Peace is made through it. Two reconcilings follow: of “all things,” whether in earth or in heaven, and of “you,” who were alienated and enemies. The volume reads the second as the passage’s own application to its readers. The first, “all things,” is stated as the scope of the reconciling, and the volume does not say more about it than the verse does."),
("sub", "9.4 “Justified by his blood” (Romans 5:9)"),
("p", "“Much more then, being now justified by his blood, we shall be saved from wrath through him” (Romans 5:9). The blood in Romans 5:9 stands with justification and with salvation from wrath. In Romans 3:25 it stands with propitiation and faith. In Ephesians 1:7 it stands with redemption and forgiveness. In Ephesians 2:13 with nearness. In Colossians 1:20 with peace. In none of these is the blood a single doctrine: it is in each sentence a means, and the thing it is the means of differs."),
("tbl", "The blood in Paul’s letters, passage by passage.", [
    ("Verse", "KJV text", "What to observe"),
    ("Rom 3:25", "“a propitiation through faith in his blood”", "With propitiation and faith"),
    ("Rom 5:9", "“justified by his blood”", "With justification; saved from wrath"),
    ("Eph 1:7", "“redemption through his blood, the forgiveness of sins”", "With redemption and forgiveness"),
    ("Eph 2:13", "“made nigh by the blood of Christ”", "With nearness"),
    ("Col 1:14", "“redemption through his blood, even the forgiveness of sins”", "With redemption and forgiveness"),
    ("Col 1:20", "“peace through the blood of his cross”", "With peace and the cross"),
  ], [0.14, 0.50, 0.36]),
("sub", "9.5 The blood and the new covenant"),
("p", "The Scripture speaks of the blood of a new covenant, and the cup of the Supper is named in it. That is the subject of the study on the Lord’s Supper, where Paul’s words to the Corinthians on the cup are read. The study on the new covenant sets out that the new covenant belongs to the house of Israel and the house of Judah and that the Body is not a party to it. This volume holds to that. The blood passages in Romans, Ephesians and Colossians are read as the Body’s own passages. They do not say that the Body is brought into Israel’s covenant, and they do not say that the Body’s benefit from the blood is a covenant benefit. They say that the Body has redemption, forgiveness, nearness and peace through the blood."),
("p", "This is not a small distinction. It means that the volume reads the word blood as a means, in each sentence, of a stated result, and not as a name for a covenant. It also means that when the reader moves from Ephesians 1:7 to a passage in another part of the Bible where the blood is named with a covenant, the reader has moved to a different sentence, and the volume asks that the sentence be read for itself."),
]
